breaking overnight the u.s. and South  Korea are analyzing North Korea's launch  of several projectiles the South Korean  military says they were launched from  North Korea's East Coast and flew  several miles before landing in the  ocean Elizabeth Palmer is in Tokyo  tracking developments Liz good morning  at about nine o'clock this morning North  Korea fired several what's being called  projectiles from a base near the East  Coast town of one son the Pentagon and  arms experts are now studying satellite  data to pinpoint exactly what these  weapons were but the evidence so far  suggests that they were short-range  rockets that flew about a hundred and  twenty five miles out over the sea  unlike the long-range ballistic missiles  the North Koreans tested in 2017 these  Rockets cannot threaten the United  States however they could hit South  Korea there's no doubt the launch is a  message it's not so clear who it's  addressed to the North Korean regime may  be signaling its frustration with the US  over stalled negotiations to swap  sanctions relief for denuclearization  talks between president Trump and kim  jeong-hoon ended in a stalemate last  February the launch may also be designed  to show North Korea's anger with the  South Koreans for participating in two  weeks of joint military exercises with  the US Air Force the White House has  responded to the news with a brief  statement saying it was aware of North  Korea's actions and would continue to  monitor as necessary Japan's foreign  minister spoke with Secretary of State  Mike Pompeo today and confirmed that the  North Korean launch didn't pose a  security risk to his country but of  course all the countries in this region  will be watching very closely to see if  this is just the beginning of even more  provocative actions by North Korea  Anthony Elizabeth Palmer thank you Liz
 

